# Visual Analytics on a Motor Insurance Portfolio {.center background-color="#2E86AB"}

Take-home Exercise 1 · Executive Summary Your Name · 24 May 2026

::: notes
This 15-slide executive summary presents a visual-analytics study of a real Spanish motor insurance portfolio comprising 354,140 policy-years between 2022 and 2024. The work answers two questions the brief asks: how are numerical and categorical variables distributed, and which factors drive profitability and volatility? Every chart was produced in R using tidyverse for data wrangling and ggplot2 plus extensions for visualisation. The narrative emphasises insight over breadth: a small number of well-chosen charts replaces dozens of dashboard tiles. I will conclude with concrete pricing and segmentation recommendations supported by statistical tests. Throughout, I use the aggregated loss ratio rather than the mean of policy ratios because the latter is dominated by the 88% of policies that have no claim.
:::

## Contents {.smaller}

1.  Context & objectives
2.  Data overview and wrangling
3.  Univariate analysis — numerical variables
4.  Univariate analysis — portfolio mix
5.  Profitability — loss ratio trend
6.  Profitability — policy type
7.  Profitability — behavioural drivers
8.  Profitability — heatmap & brand
9.  Volatility — severity distribution
10. Volatility — loss concentration
11. Statistical validation
12. Synthesis & recommendations
13. References

::: notes
The deck is organised in three movements. Slides 3 and 4 establish what the portfolio looks like through univariate exploration. Slides 5 through 8 isolate the profitability question, moving from the macro trend down to the segment that is driving the deterioration. Slides 9 and 10 then pivot to volatility, focusing on severity and loss concentration. Slide 11 brings statistical tests in to confirm what the visuals suggest. Slide 12 synthesises actionable findings. I have kept text minimal on every slide because each chart is designed to communicate its insight without needing narration, freeing the speaker notes for interpretation rather than description.
:::

## Context & objectives

> "Insurers often carry underperforming portfolios where the root causes of poor profitability and high volatility are unknown."

::: incremental
- **Data**: open-access Spanish motor portfolio, 354,140 policy-years, 47 variables, 2022–2024
- **Stack**: tidyverse + ggplot2 family (ggridges, ggdist, ggstatsplot, patchwork)
- **Goal**: visually identify and statistically validate drivers of underperformance
:::

::: notes
This study addresses a common problem in non-life insurance: aggregated KPIs mask the heterogeneous behaviour of sub-portfolios. The McKinsey quote on the slide frames the practical motivation. The dataset is a real motor-vehicle book published on Mendeley Data and is large enough to support segmentation analysis without running into power problems. I work entirely in R because the tidyverse offers a coherent grammar for wrangling and ggplot2 plus its extensions cover every visual pattern I need, from histograms and ridges to statistical annotation and concentration curves. The deliverables are this revealjs deck for stakeholders plus a longer reproducible HTML technical report that documents every transformation and design choice.
:::

